Command Line Arguments
•Generally main( ) function takes no arguments
•main( ) function can take special arguments and parameters are to be 
passed to the main from operating system
•Two arguments are passed to the main( ) function
-argc : integer type representing the number of arguments passed
-argv : array of strings, each string representing a parameter passed to main ( )
•Execution of the program is initiated by specifying the name of the program followed 
by the parameters separated by space
•The program name is interpreted as an operating system command and the line in 
which it appears is called command line
•As the arguments are provided at command line, they are called command line 
arguments
1
Example program 1: Command line arguments
•#include < stdio.h >
•int main( int argc , char * argv [ ])
•{
• int i;
• for (i= 0; i< argc ; i++) 
• printf (“%s \n”, argv [i]);
• return 0;
• }
•At the command line, the following command can be given
•C:\> claprog first second third
•argv [0] is the program name claprog , argv [1]  is first argument first,
•Argv [2] is second argument second and argv [3] is third argument third
