Functions with Variable number of Argument in C
•Variable length arguments is a feature that allows a function to receive 
any number of arguments.  
•printf is an example of a function with variable number of arguments
•Example problems where they are useful
•1) Sum of given numbers 2) Minimum of given numbers
•Variable number of arguments are represented by three dots (…) or ellipsis
•The include file < stdarg.h > is to be used for the three macros  va_list ,          
va_start and va_arg that are useful to deal with variable arguments
va_start is used to initialize a pointer to the beginning of the list of optional arguments
va_arg is used to advance the pointer to the next argument
va_list holds the information about the arguments
11
Differences between Macros and functions
•A macro is a list of code with a name
•Whenever the name is used, it is replaced by the code
Macros Functions
•Before compilation, macro name               During function call, transfer
is replaced with a list of code                             of control takes place
•Macros are useful when small code           Functions are useful when large
is to be repeated many times                      code is to be written
•The macros are expanded by                    The inline functions are parsed    
the preprocessor by the compiler; inline function
can access class  members 
Macros are efficient and faster                  There is overhead of taking control
as the code is directly replaced              from function call to called function            
